          %h2#GeneralIdea Idea
          %p
            %em> Functorial quantum field theory
            , or FQFT for short, is one of two approaches to providing a precise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematical
            formulation and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            (generalized to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            are assigned to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            and where linear maps are assigned to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            interpolating between these spaces.
          %h3#as_an_axiomatization_of_the_path_integral_and_smatrix As an axiomatization of the path integral and S-Matrix
          %p
            The axioms of FQFT may be understood as formulating the basic properties that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            invoked in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            ought to satisfy, if they had been given a precise definition.
          %p
            Much work in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is based on arguments invoking the concept of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            = succeed "." do
              %em sewing laws
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            which decomposes into subdomains
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
            is the same as the path integral over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_1
            composed with that over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
          %p
            Accordingly, it is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            that is manifestly incarnated in the Atiyah-Segal picture of functorial QFT:
          %p
            Here, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                    %mi S
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z \colon Bord_d^S \longrightarrow Vect
          %p
            from a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+cobordisms"} category of cobordisms
            to a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                To a codimension-1 slice
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{d-1}
                of space, this assigns a vector space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(M_{d-1})
                – the (Hilbert)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
                over
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi M
                          %mrow
                            %mi d
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{d-1}
            %li
              %p
                to a
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundaries"} boundaries
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi in
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi out
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(M) : Z(\partial_{in} M) \to Z(\partial_{out} M)
                that takes incoming states to outgoing states via propagation along the spacetime/worldvolume
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(M)
                is alternatively known as the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} scattering amplitude
                or
                %em S-matrix
                for propagation from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi in
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_{in}M
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi out
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_{out}M
                along a process of shape
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %p
            For genuine
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theories"} topological field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            and hence we can equivalently consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual vector space
            (using that finite dimensional vector spaces form a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi in
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi out
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z(M) : Z(\partial_{in}M) \to Z(\partial_{out}M)
          %p equivalently becomes a linear map of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi out
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi in
                  %mi M
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{C} \to Z(\partial_{out}M) \otimes Z(\partial_{in}M)^\ast = Z(\partial M)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Notice that such a linear map from the canonical 1-dimensional complex vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            to some other vector space is equivalently just a choice of an element in that vector space. It is in this sense that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(M)
            is equivalently a vector in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi out
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi in
                    %mi M
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(\partial_{out}M) \otimes Z(\partial_{in}M)^\ast = Z(\partial M)
          %p
            In this form in physics, the propagator is usually called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"} correlator
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} n-point function
            \.
          %p
            The axioms of (
            %a{:href => "#Segal04"}> Segal 04
            ) for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            in this case) were originally explicitly about the propagators/S-matrices, while (
            %a{:href => "#Atiyah88"}> Atiyah 88
            ) formulated it in terms of the correlators. Both perspectives go over into each other under duality as above.
          %p
            Notice that this kind of discussion is not restricted to topological field theory. For instance, plain quantum mechanics is usefully formulated this way—that is the point of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} finite quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
          %h3#as_the_dual_axiomatization_to_aqft As the dual axiomatization to AQFT
          %p
            Historically older is the proposal for axiomatizing QFT that is known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            = succeed "." do
              %em algebraic quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebras"} factorization algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            to regions of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            In principle,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            and FQFT should be two sides of the same coin, and in special cases this has been made precise (see for instance
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+chiral+homology"} topological chiral homology
          %div
            %p
