            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} locality
                  of time evolution
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          %h4#entanglement_and_monoidal_structure Entanglement and Monoidal structure
          %p
            Besides the time evolution, there is the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composite+systems"} composite systems
          %p
            Given two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+systems"} quantum mechanical systems
            (e.g. of two electrons orbiting the same atomic nucleus), with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+states"}> pure states
            )
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of vector spaces
            (of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Vect
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V \otimes \tilde V \in Vect
                  \\,.
          %p
            This should be compared with the way compound systems are formed in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_2
            the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+spaces"} configuration spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            of two classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanical+systems"} mechanical systems
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \times X_2
          %p
            These Cartesian products and tensor products extend to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon V \to V
            is a linear operator acting on the first system and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f \colon \tilde V\to \tilde V
            is one acting on the second system, then there is a tensor product morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (f_1\otimes f_2)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  V \otimes \tilde V
                  \\longrightarrow
                  V \otimes \tilde V
                  \\,.
          %p
            Hence (Cartesian or non-cartesian)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+products"} tensor products
            are something like product operations on sets, but on whole
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            of vector spaces when equipped with the tensor product of vector spaces is a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %p
            Similarly, the categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            or just bare
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of sets. This is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal structure
          %p
            The characteristic property of Cartesian products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \times X_2
            is that elements of these are equivalently pairs of elements in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_2
          %p
            The tensor product on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            however is not Cartesian: an element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi \in V_1 \otimes V_2
            need not be of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_1\otimes \psi_2
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_i \in V_i
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
            of such elements
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi j
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi j
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Psi = \underset{j}{\sum}  (\Psi_1)_j \otimes (\Psi_2)_j
                  \\,.
          %p
            In terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            such non-cartesian vectors are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            that exhibit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entanglement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Vect,\otimes)
            being a non-cartesian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entanglement
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} non-cartesian
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
          %p
            For more exposition of this point see (
            %a{:href => "#Baez04"}> Baez 04
            ).
          %h4#brakets_and_dual_objects Bra-Kets and Dual objects
          %p
            Consider now for simplicity of notation an application in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            involved are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite dimensional vector spaces
            (spaces of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qubits"} qubits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+Hall+effect"} quantum Hall system
          %p
            Then for every space of states
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            there is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            notation the states in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kets"} kets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert \Psi \rangle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^\ast
            are the “bra”s
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \Psi \vert
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bra"}> bra
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ket"} ket
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
          %p
            Essentially all of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            has a slick reformulation in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} with dual objects
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} finite quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
          %p
            While the introduction of bra-ket notation by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Dirac"} Paul Dirac
            was (while just notation) already quite useful for thinking about the subject, the language of monoidal categories in fact reflects the actual physical processes involved even better.
          %p
            For instance, in quantum mechanics textbooks one often sees the following manipulation of symbols for expressing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            in terms of a sum over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis+of+a+vector+space"} basis
            elements
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                            %mfrac
                              %mi i
                              %mi ℏ
                          %mi H
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                            %mfrac
                              %mi i
                              %mi ℏ
                          %mi H
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %msub
                              %mi Ψ
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %msub
                              %mi Ψ
                              %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mo )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                            %mfrac
                              %mi i
                              %mi ℏ
                          %mi H
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %msub
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %msub
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi Ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %msub
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi exp
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                              %mfrac
                                %mi i
                                %mi ℏ
                            %mi H
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %msub
                            %mi Ψ
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\langle \Psi \vert \exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} H t ) \vert \Psi \rangle
                  \&amp; =
                  \\langle \Psi \vert \exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} H t )
                  \\left( \sum_j \vert \Psi_j \rangle \langle \Psi_j \vert \right)
                  \\vert \Psi \rangle
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  \\sum_j
                  \\langle \Psi \vert \exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} H t ) \vert \Psi_j \rangle
                  \\langle \Psi_j \vert \Psi \rangle
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = \sum_j
                  \\langle \Psi_j \vert
                  \\left(
                  \\vert \Psi \rangle
                  \\langle \Psi \vert \exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} H t )
                  \\right)
                  \\vert \Psi_j \rangle
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This “rotation” operation where symbols are cyclically permuted reflects the fact that indeed traces as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+functions"} partition functions
            reflect actual physical circular processes.
          %p
            In “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"}> string diagram
            ”-notation of
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ↦
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  1 \mapsto \underset{j}{\sum}  \vert \Psi_j \rangle \langle \Psi_j \vert
          %p is depicted as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟵
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi V
                            %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \longleftarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow
                  \\\
                  V &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; V^\ast
                  }
          %p and the counit map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vert \Psi_j \rangle \langle \Psi_k \vert
                  \\mapsto
                  \\langle \Psi_k \vert \Psi_j \rangle
          %p as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi V
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; V^\ast
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \longrightarrow
                  }
                  \\,.
          %h4#partition_functions_and_monoidal_string_diagrams Partition functions and Monoidal string diagrams
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi t
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi Tr
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi exp
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mfrac
                          %mi i
                          %mi ℏ
                      %mi H
                      %mi t
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z_t \coloneqq Tr\left(  \exp\left(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} H t \right)  \right)
                  \\,.
          %p In bra-ket notation this is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi j
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mi j
                    %mo |
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mi ℏ
                    %mi H
                    %mi t
                    %mo )
                  %mrow
                    %mo |
                    %msub
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mi j
                    %mo ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z_t = \underset{j}{\sum}
                  \\left\langle \Psi_j \right\vert
                  \\exp\left(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} H t \right)  \left| \Psi_j \right\rangle
          %p
            In terms of monoidal category-theoretic notation (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"}> string diagrams
            ) this same expression reads as follows
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟵
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi V
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi exp
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                  %mfrac
                                    %mi i
                                    %mi ℏ
                                %mi H
                                %mi t
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi id
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi V
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \longleftarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow
                  \\\
                  V  &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; V^\ast
                  \\\
                  {}^{\mathllap{\exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} H t)}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \uparrow^{\mathrlap{id}}
                  \\\
                  V &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; V^\ast
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \longrightarrow
                  }
          %p
            This is striking, because this
            %em picture
            is an accurate reflection of the physical process that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            describes, for the partition function is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"} correlator
            of a particle with a closed circular
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
          %p
            In fact, the monoidal category theoretic
            = succeed "-notation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            = succeed "-notation." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#QMWithInteractionAndFeynmanDiagrams"} below
          %p
            For a 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            the Hamiltonian above vanishes. (Or maybe more interestingly: for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            the Hamiltonian may not vanish, but in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+trace"} super trace
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            all non-zero energy eigenmodes cancel out by supersymmetry, and only the topological part is left after all.)
          %p In this case the partition function reduces to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟵
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi V
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \longleftarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow
                  \\\
                  V &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; V^\ast
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \longrightarrow
                  }
          %p
            which is just the trace on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            To reflect this in the functorial notation from
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#TimeEvolutionAndFunctors"} above
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Riem
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_{1}^{Riem}
            from above is naturally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            if we take its objects to consist not just of single points, but of arbitrary collections of points, and to have its morphisms consist of all 1-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Riem
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Bord_1^{Riem})^\coprod
            is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
          %p
            A 1d TQFT with values in vector spaces is then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Riem
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (Bord_1^{Riem})^\coprod \longrightarrow Vect^{\otimes}
                  \\,.
          %p Other processes that such a 1d TQFT encodes include
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi V
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟵
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi V
                            %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; V^\ast
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \longrightarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \longleftarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow
                  \\\
                  V &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; V^\ast
                  }
          %p
            In this way a 1d TQFT is entirely encoded in the operation that exhibit a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+object"} dualizable object
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{
            1d TQFT with coefficients in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \}
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+objects"} dualizable objects
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \}
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite dimensional vector spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \}
          %p
            This is the simplest incarnation of the statement that for higher dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFT"} extended TQFT
            becomes the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#IntroductionGeneralFormulation"} below
          %h3#PathIntegralQuantizationof1dGaugeTheory Path integral quantization of 1d Gauge theory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            tends to be as suggestive in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            as it is invoked ubiquitously, and FQFT may be understood as being precisely the axiomatics that a would-be path integral ought to satisfy, thereby decoupling its construction as a suitably regularized integral from its operational definition as yielding a consistent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            for the QFT.
          %p
            We discuss now the simplest non-trivial example of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+model"} Dijkgraaf-Witten model
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                indicating how not just the final quantum theory but also its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum data
                is naturally organized by monoidal category theory;
            %li
              %p
                motivating and introducing elements of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
                which become crucial for the understanding of FQFT as one moves up in dimension.
          %p
            A more detailed version of this section is at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "prequantum+field+theory#1dDWTheory"} Local prequantum field theory – 1d Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
          %h4#GroupoidsAndBasicHomotopy1TypeTheory Gauge transformation and Groupoids
          %p
            The following is a quick review of basics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-type"}> homotopy 1-type
            \-theory), geared towards the constructions and facts needed for 1-dimensional Dijkgraaf-Witten theory.
          #Groupoid.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"}> small
              )
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
              is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a pair of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_0 \in Set
                  (the set of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_1 \in Set
                  (the set of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              %li
                %p
                  equipped with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi 𝒢
                                  %mn 1
                                %msub
                                  %mo ×
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi 𝒢
                                      %mn 0
                                %msub
                                  %mi 𝒢
                                  %mn 1
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo ⟶
                                  %mo ∘
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi 𝒢
                                  %mn 1
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mover
                                    %munder
                                      %mo ⟶
                                      %mi s
                                    %mover
                                      %mo ←
                                      %mi i
                                  %mover
                                    %mo ⟶
                                    %mi t
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi 𝒢
                                  %mn 0
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        \\mathcal{G}_1 \times_{\mathcal{G}_0} \mathcal{G}_1
                        \&amp;\stackrel{\circ}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                        \\mathcal{G}_1
                        \&amp; \stackrel{\overset{t}{\longrightarrow}}{\stackrel{\overset{i}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{s}{\longrightarrow}}}&amp;
                        \\mathcal{G}_0
                        }\,,
                %p
                  where the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
                  on the left is that over
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒢
                            %mn 1
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi t
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒢
                            %mn 0
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mi s
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒢
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_1 \stackrel{t}{\to} \mathcal{G}_0 \stackrel{s}{\leftarrow} \mathcal{G}_1
            %p such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  takes values in
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphisms"} endomorphisms
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi id
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝒢
                              %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        t \circ i = s \circ i =   id_{\mathcal{G}_0}, \;\;\;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∘
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
                  defines a partial
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
                  operation which is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unital
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(\mathcal{G}_0)
                  the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identities"} identities
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s (g \circ f) = s(f)
                  and
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi g
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi g
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t (g \circ f) = t(g)
              %li
                %p
                  every morphism has an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
                  under this composition.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p This data is visualized as follows. The set of morphisms is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 0
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi k
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{G}_1
                    \=
                    \\left\{
                    \\phi_0 \stackrel{k}{\to} \phi_1
                    \\right\}
            %p and the set of pairs of composable morphisms is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 1
                    %munder
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mn 1
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mrow
                                %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi k
                                      %mn 1
                              %mo ↗
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo ↘
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi k
                                      %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mn 0
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi k
                                    %mn 2
                                  %mo ∘
                                  %msub
                                    %mi k
                                    %mn 1
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mn 2
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{G}_2 \coloneqq \mathcal{G}_1 \underset{\mathcal{G}_0}{\times} \mathcal{G}_1
                    \=
                    \\left\{
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \phi_1
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{k_1}}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{k_2}}
                    \\\
                    \\phi_0 &amp;&amp; \stackrel{k_2 \circ k_1}{\to} &amp;&amp; \phi_2
                    }
                    \\right\}
                    \\,.
            %p
              The functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1, p_2, \circ \colon \mathcal{G}_2 \to \mathcal{G}_1
              are those which send, respectively, these triangular diagrams to the left morphism, or the right morphism, or the bottom morphism.
          #SetAsGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to be the set of objects and adding only precisely the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
              morphism from each object to itself
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mover
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi id
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟵
                            %mi id
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi id
                      %mi X
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    X
                    \\stackrel
                    {\overset{id}{\longrightarrow}}
                    {
                    \\stackrel{\overset{id}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{id}{\longrightarrow}}
                    }
                    X
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          #DeloopingGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}G)_\bullet
              has
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi G
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}G)_0 = \ast
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi G
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 1
                        %mo =
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}G)_1 = G
                  \.
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              two groups, group homomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon G \to K
              are in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+bijection"} natural bijection
              with groupoid homomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi K
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbf{B}f)_\bullet
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (\mathbf{B}G)_\bullet \to (\mathbf{B}K)_\bullet
                    \\,.
            %p
              In particular a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+character"} group character
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \colon G \to U(1)
              is equivalently a groupoid homomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi c
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbf{B}c)_\bullet
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (\mathbf{B}G)_\bullet
                    \\to
                    (\mathbf{B}U(1))_\bullet
                    \\,.
            %p
              Here, for the time being, all groups are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+groups"} discrete groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
              also has a standard structure of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♭
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\flat U(1) \in Grp
            %p
              to mean explicitly the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete group
              underlying the circle group. (Here “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♭
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
              ” denotes the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"}> flat modality
              ”.)
          #ActionGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete group
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \colon X \times G \to X
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+representation"} permutation representation
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
              or
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is the groupoid
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ρ
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi G
                      %mover
                        %munder
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi p
                              %mn 1
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi ρ
                      %mi X
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X//_\rho G
                    \=
                    \\left(
                    X \times G
                    \\stackrel{\overset{\rho}{\longrightarrow}}{\underset{p_1}{\longrightarrow}}
                    X
                    \\right)
            %p
              with composition induced by the product in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              are of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    x_1 \stackrel{g}{\to} x_2 = \rho(x_1)(g)
            %p
              for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, x_2 \in X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in G
          %p As an important special case we have:
          #BGGroupoidAsActionGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete"} discrete
              group and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              the trivial action of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              on the point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              (the singleton set), the coresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
              according to def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ActionGroupoid"}
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              groupoid of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              according to def.
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DeloopingGroupoid"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\ast //G)_\bullet = (\mathbf{B}G)_\bullet
                    \\,.
            %p
              Another canonical action is the action of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              on itself by right multiplication. The corresponding action groupoid we write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbf{E}G)_\bullet \coloneqq G//G
                    \\,.
            %p
              The constant map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \to \ast
              induces a canonical morphism
            .maruku-equation
