              on the right sends a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+configuration"} field configuration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in G = [\Pi(S^1), \mathbf{B}G]_0
              to its value
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ♭
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(g) \in U(1) = (\flat \mathbf{B}U(1))_1
              under the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+character"} group character
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              It follows that in a discussion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
              of 1d DW theory is given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schur+inner+product"} Schur integral
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+character"} group character
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi G
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{1}{\vert G \vert}
                    \\underset{g\in G}{\sum}
                    c(g)
                    \=
                    \\langle c,1\rangle
                    \\,.
          %p
            In conclusion,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-dimensional+Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} 1-dimensional Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            as a prequantum field theory comes down to be essentially a geometric interpretation of what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+characters"} group characters
            are and do. One may regard this as a simple example of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+representation+theory"} geometric representation theory
          %h3#QMWithInteractionAndFeynmanDiagrams Quantum mechanics with interactions and Feynman diagrams
          %p
            We saw above that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            theory naturally captures all the key aspects of basic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            This becomes all the more pronounced when one considers quantum mechanics with interaction as in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            and hence when one considers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            as diagrams of interactions of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
          %h4#spectral_triples_and_graph_representations Spectral triples and Graph representations
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Cob
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 1
                    %mi Feyn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Cob_{1|1}^{Feyn}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+category"} cobordism category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+graphs"} Feynman graphs
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superparticle"} superparticle
            with a single type of interaction along the lines of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+K-theory"} (1,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and K-theory
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1|1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\bullet
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \bullet &amp; \to
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  \\bullet
                  }
          %p subject to the obvious associativity condition.
          %p
            Then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,H,D)
            is the data encoding a sufficiently nice smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi H
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi R
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Cob
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 1
                    %mi Feyn
                  %mo →
                  %mi sVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z_{(A,H,D)} : R Cob_{1|1}^{Feyn}  \to sVect
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
          %p Here
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi H
                          %mo ,
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo •
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = Z_{(A,H,D)}(\bullet)_0
                is the even part of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
                assigned by the functor to the point, equipped with the structure of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                whose product is given by the image of the interaction vertex
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi H
                          %mo ,
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo •
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↘
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo •
                              %mtd
                                %mo →
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↗
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo •
                        %mo )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Z_{(A,H,D)}
                      \\left(
                      \\array{
                      \\bullet
                      \\\
                      \&amp; \searrow
                      \\\
                      \&amp;&amp; \bullet &amp; \to
                      \\\
                      \&amp; \nearrow
                      \\\
                      \\bullet
                      }
                      \\right)
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                is some completion of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi H
                          %mo ,
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_{(A,H,D)}(\bullet)
                to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Hilbert+space"} super Hilbert space
            %li
              %p
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi End
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \in End(H)
                is an odd self-adjoint operator on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo ,
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t,\theta)
                by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo ,
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi t
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %mi θ
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      (t,\theta) \mapsto \exp( - t D^2 + \theta D )
                      \\,.
          %p
            So this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superparticle"} superparticle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            = succeed "in" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi L
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = L^2(S)
                is the space of square integrable spinor sections;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = C^\infty(X)
                is the space of smooth functions on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            One point of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            is to take the view of world-line
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            as basic and
            %em characterize
            the spin Riemannian geometry of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            entirely by this algebraic data. In particular the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+spectrum"} operator spectrum
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            Then with all the ordinary geoemtry re-encoded algebraically this way, in terms of the 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that
            %em probes
            this geometry, one can then use the same formulas to interpret spectral triple geometrically that do
            %em not
            come from an ordinary geometry as in the above example.
          %h4#feynman_diagram_in_worldline_formalism_and_monoidal_string_diagrams Feynman diagram in worldline formalism and Monoidal string diagrams
          %p
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"}> worldline formalism
            …
          %p
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"}> Feynman diagram
            …
          %p
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"}> string diagram
            …
          %h3#QuantumTopologicalString Quantum topological string
          %h4#Global2dTQFT Global 2d TQFT and Frobenius algebra
          %p
            In view of the above discussion of “topological quantum mechanics”, i.e. of 1-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+cobordisms"} categories of cobordisms
            of higher dimension and consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functors"} strong monoidal functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mo ⊔
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z \;\colon\; Bord_n^\sqcup \longrightarrow Vect^\otimes
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Atiyah88"}> Atiyah 88
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Segal04"} Segal 04
          %p
            for instance 1-dimensional cobordisms with boundary describe a kind
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+TQFT"} 2d TQFT
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary field theory
          %p
            %img{:alt => "string diagrams for the Frobenius algebra axioms", :src => "/nlab/files/frobenius_algebra.jpg"}/
          %p
            As almost immediate from these picture, such 2d TQFTs are equivalent to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebras"} Frobenius algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            In terms of physics:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states"} space of states
            of a topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
            and the algebra and coalgebra structure on it encodes its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} 3-point functions
          %p
            More generally open and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+strings"} closed strings
          %p
            %img{:alt => "diagrams for the monoid laws in 2Cob", :src => "/nlab/files/monoid_laws.jpg"}/
          %p
            Now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            is equivalent to a pair consisting of a Frobenius algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and a commutative Frobenius algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to Z(A)
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"} center
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ⊔
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Bord_2^{\sqcup} \longrightarrow Vect^\otimes
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#MooreSegal06"} Moore-Segal
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lazaroiu00"} Lazaroiu 00
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#LaudaPfeiffer05"} Lauda-Pfeiffer 05
          %p
            In physics speak
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is the space of states for the topological
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+string"} closed string
          %p
            Or rather, it is
            %em some
            topological string model, but not the one originally obtained by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+twist"} topological twist
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 2d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            which is commonly what is understood as the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"}> topological string
            ” in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"}> A-model
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"}> B-model
            ).
          %h4#Local2dTQFT
            Cohomological 2d TQFT and Calabi-Yau
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
          %p
            Curiously, the above does
            %em not
            capture the original motivating examples for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+TQFT"} 2d TQFT
            that came from physics, namely it does not capture the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomological+quantum+field+theory"}> cohomological quantum field theory
            ” due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
            in its incarnation as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Witten
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomological+field+theory"} cohomological field theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
            %li
              %p
                Kontsevich:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
                is equivalence of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+categories"} A-∞ categories
            %li
              %p
                Aspinwall, Douglas et al: the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+categories"} derived categories
                here are those of topological
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-branes"} A-branes
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-branes"}> A-branes
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-branes"}> B-branes
                )
          %p
            Hence need to regard
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            open
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
            as having a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            but an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} A-∞ algebra
            with suitable trace.
          %p
            Examples come from twisting the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+%282%2C0%29-CFT"} 2d (2,0)-CFT
            induced from a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+A-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} Calabi-Yau A-∞ algebra
          %p
            remember space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
          %ul
            %li
              Getzler, Segal: “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"}> TCFT
              ”
          %p
            classification by (
            %a{:href => "#Costello04"}> Costello 04
            ) sums it up:
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+A-%E2%88%9E+category"} Calabi-Yau A-∞ category
            is equivalent to non-compact open topological string with coefficients in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch(Vect)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of the category are the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-spaces"} hom-spaces
            are the spaces of quantum states of open strings stretching between these. The closed string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk+field+theory"} bulk field theory
            sector is given by forming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+category"} A-∞ category
            refinement (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+triangulated+category"} enhanced triangulated category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category+of+coherent+sheaves"} derived category of coherent sheaves
            is an example.
          %h4#local_2d_tqft_and_2modules Local 2d TQFT and 2-Modules
          %p
            Given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
