                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \longleftarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \nwarrow
                  \\\
                  V &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; V^\otimes
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \longrightarrow
                  }
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\Rightarrow
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\ast
          %p
            One may view this as exhibiting “higher order duality”: where the semi-circles exhibits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} dual object
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+morphism"} adjoint
            to the lower semicircle.
          %p (…)
          %h3#IntroductionGeneralFormulation General local TQFT
          %h4#covariant_quantization_and_directed_homotopy_types Covariant quantization and Directed homotopy types
          %p
            One way to understand from the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            why the 1-functorial description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+TQFT"} 2d TQFT
            %a{:href => "#Global2dTQFT"} above
            is unsatisfactory is that it breaks what is known as “covariance” in physics, in the sense of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"}> general covariance
            ” (reflected also in the term “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"}> covariant phase space
            ”): implicit in the concept of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+cobordisms"} category of cobordisms
            is a splitting of a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolumes"} worldvolumes
            into spatial slices (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            between these.
          %p
            The standard
            = succeed "-data" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theories"} topological quantum field theories
            are supposed to arise under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            do not enforce such a splitting as indeed they are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} generally covariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
            should, after
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
            that is still “generally covariant” in that it does not require or depend on such a splitting. In physics this plays a crucial role for instance in considerations related to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
          %p
            We saw
            %a{:href => "#CorrespondencesOfGroupoids"} above
            how 1-dimensional (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"}> prequantum
            ) field theory is encoded by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Fields
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi Δ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Fields
                          %mo ×
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Fields
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{Fields}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{\Delta}}
                  \\\
                  \\ast &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; \mathbf{Fields} \times \mathbf{Fields}
                  }
          %p and the reverse process of them disappearing is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
