          %p
            To describe local propagation in higher dimensional field theory this way, evidently we need a higher dimensional calculus that deals both with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"}> gauge theory
            ) involves as well as with the directionality of these processes.
          %p
            We already saw the first hint of how this works:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            above appeared in two different guises, on the one hand as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"} homotopy 1-types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            with directed morphisms.
          %p
            Now
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"} homotopy 1-types
            have a classical generalization to general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            regarded up to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
          %p
            A crucial fact is that one may pair this full homotopy-theoretic aspect with the category-theoretic aspect to get
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} ∞-categories
          .maruku-equation
