                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
          %h2#references References
          %h3#terminology Terminology
          %p
            The idea of functorial field theory originates with the unpublished precursor note of
            %a{:href => "#Segal04"} Segal (2004)
            and became popular (in the special case of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Atiyah88"} Atiyah (1988)
          %p
            The term
            %em functorial quantum field theory
            appears to originate around June 2008 with
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Schreiber09"} Schreiber (2009)
          %p At some point later, the adjective “quantum” was dropped because the formalism also encodes classical and prequantum field theories.
          %h3#formalization_of_sewing_and_locality_in_terms_of_functoriality Formalization of sewing and locality in terms of functoriality
          %p It was in
          %ul
            %li#Atiyah88
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topological quantum field theory
          %p that it was realized that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                this means that this property can be taken as the
                %em defining
                property of the path integral, thereby circumventing the problem of constructing it as an actual integral;
            %li
              %p
                this property can be conveniently axiomatized by saying that the path integral is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                from a suitable category whose morphisms are
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
          %p (Strictly speaking, Atiyah’s original article mentions this functor slightly indirectly only.)
          %p
            All this was originally formalized in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} topological quantum field theory
            only. This is the easiest case that already exhibits all the functoriality that is implied by “FQFT” but by far not the only case (see below).
          %p A pedagogical exposition of how the physicist’s way of thinking about the path integral leads to its definition as a functor is given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Walker"}> Kevin Walker
              ,
              %em TQFTs
              (
              %a{:href => "http://canyon23.net/math/tc.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A pedagogical exposition of the notion of quantum field theory as a functor on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Baez04
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              %em Quantum quandaries: a Category-Theoretic perspective
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0404040"}> arXiv
              )
          %p and a review of much of the existing material in the literature is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Bartlett"}> Bruce Bartlett
              ,
              %em Categorical Aspects of Topological Quantum Field Theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512103"}> arXiv
              ).
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Stolz14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephan+Stolz"}> Stephan Stolz
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topology and Field Theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/conm-613"} ams:conm-613
          %p
            A survey of some further developments and conjectures (especially as related to the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AGT+correspondence"} AGT correspondence
          %ul
            %li#Tachikawa17
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuji+Tachikawa"}> Yuji Tachikawa
              ,
              %em On ‘categories’ of quantum field theories
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.09456"}> arXiv:1712.09456
              )
          %p The discussion of the open-closed case of 2d TQFT goes back to
          %ul
            %li#MooreSegal06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"}> Greg Moore
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
                %em D-branes and K-theory in 2D topological field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0609042"}> arXiv:hep-th/0609042
                )
            %li#Lazaroiu00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calin+Lazaroiu"}> Calin Lazaroiu
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the structure of open-closed topological field theory in two dimensions
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0010269"} arXiv:hep-th/0010269
          %p A picture-rich discussion is in
          %ul
            %li#LaudaPfeiffer05
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aaron+Lauda"}> Aaron Lauda
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hendryk+Pfeiffer"} Hendryk Pfeiffer
              = succeed "," do
                %em Open-closed strings: two-dimensional extended TQFTs and Frobenius algebras
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AT/0510664"} arXiv:math.AT/0510664
          %h3#ReferencesTwistedRelativeFunctorialFieldTheory Twisted/relative functorial field theory
          %p
            The notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+functorial+field+theory"} twisted
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+functorial+field+theory"} relative
            functorial field theory as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            between FQFTs was popularized in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephan+Stolz"}> Stephan Stolz
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Twisted field theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Supersymmetric field theories and generalized cohomology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
                (eds.):
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 83
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.0189"} arXiv:1108.0189
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/pspum-83/"} ams:pspum-83
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relative quantum field theory
                %strong 326
                (2014) 459–476 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.1692"}> arXiv:1212.1692
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-013-1880-1"} doi:10.1007/s00220-013-1880-1
          %p but the observation of the phenomenon is already in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                \: “A kind of Holography”, section 4 of:
                %em On 2D QFT – from Arrows to Disks
                (2006) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schreiber/ad.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "/schreiber/files/Schreiber-ArrowsToDisks.pdf"} pdf
              %p presented as:
              %p
                %em> Quantum 2-States: Sections of 2-vector bundles
                , talk at
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/programs/scientific/06-07/homotopy/highercat/index.html"} Higher categories and their applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schreiber/atd.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "/schreiber/files/schreiber_2VectorSpaces_Jan2007.pdf"} pdf
              %p
                which
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/06/extended_quantum_field_theory.html"} was communicated
                to the above authors at:
              %p
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "https://www.zmp.uni-hamburg.de/events/workshop-cohomology.html"} Workshop on Elliptic Cohomology
                , Hamburg (June 2007) &amp;lbrack;poster:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.zmp.uni-hamburg.de/events/workshop-cohomology/files/workshop-ellcoh-cdr.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/WorkshopEllipticCohomologyPoster-Hamburg2007.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p and then in:
          %ul
            %li#SchommerPries
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Schommer-Pries"}> Chris Schommer-Pries
              , pp. 65 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topological defects and classifying local topological field theories in low dimension
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oberwolfach+Workshop%2C+June+2009+--+Strings%2C+Fields%2C+Topology"} Oberwolfach Workshop, June 2009 – Strings, Fields, Topology
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SchommerPriesDefects.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Further mathematical discussion:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theo+Johnson-Freyd"}> Theo Johnson-Freyd
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudia+Scheimbauer"} Claudia Scheimbauer
                = succeed "," do
                  %em (Op)lax natural transformations, twisted quantum field theories, and “even higher” Morita categories
                %strong 307
                (2017) 147-223 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.06526"}> arXiv:1502.06526
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2016.11.014"} doi:10.1016/j.aim.2016.11.014
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudia+Scheimbauer"}> Claudia Scheimbauer
                , Thomas Stempfhuber:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relative field theories via relative dualizability
                %strong 115
                65 (2025) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05051"}> arXiv:2312.05051
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11005-025-01948-7"} doi;10.1007/s11005-025-01948-7
          %p
            Meanwhile the perspective has become popular in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theoretical+physics"} theoretical physics
            under various names such as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} anomaly
            field theory”, for instance:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Monnier"}> Samuel Monnier
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Modern Point of View on Anomalies
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Structures+in+M-Theory+2018"} Higher Structures in M-Theory 2018
              %strong 67
              8-9 (2019) 1910012 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02828"}> arXiv:1903.02828
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/prop.201910012"} doi:10.1002/prop.201910012
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/PAKf_o5XCek"} yt
          %p and “symmetry TFT”, for instance:
          %ul
            %li
              Fabio Apruzzi,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Federico+Bonetti"} Federico Bonetti
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sakura+Sch%C3%A4fer-Nameki"} Sakura Schäfer-Nameki
              = succeed "," do
                %em Symmetry TFTs from String Theory
              %strong 402
              (2023) 895–949 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.02092"}> arXiv:2112.02092
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-023-04737-2"} doi:10.1007/s00220-023-04737-2
          %div
            %h3#ReferencesRelationBetweenAQFTAndFQFT Relation between algebraic and functorial field theory
            %p
              On the relation between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+quantum+field+theory"} functorial quantum field theory
              (axiomatizing the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
              of
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
              (axiomatizing the
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
            %ul
              %li#Schreiber09
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/files/AQFTfromFQFT.pdf", :title => "AQFT from n-Functorial QFT"} AQFT from n-Functorial QFT
                  %strong 291
                  2 (2009) 357-401 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-009-0840-2"}> doi:10.1007/s00220-009-0840-2
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1079"} arXiv:0806.1079
              %li#Johnson-Freyd21
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theo+Johnson-Freyd"}> Theo Johnson-Freyd
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Heisenberg-picture quantum field theory
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Representation Theory, Mathematical Physics, and Integrable Systems
                  %strong 340
                  (2021) [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-78148-4_13"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-030-78148-4_13
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.05908"} arXiv:1508.05908
              %li#BunkMacManusSchenkel23
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Severin+Bunk"}> Severin Bunk
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+MacManus"} James MacManus
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"} Alexander Schenkel
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Lorentzian bordisms in algebraic quantum field theory
                  %strong 115
                  16 (2025) [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11005-025-01906-3"}> doi:10.1007/s11005-025-01906-3
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.01026"} arXiv:2308.01026
              %li#BunkMacManusSchenkel25
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Severin+Bunk"}> Severin Bunk
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+MacManus"} James MacManus
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"} Alexander Schenkel
                  %em An equivalence theorem for algebraic and functorial QFT
                  [
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15759"}> arXiv:2504.15759
                  ]
          %h3#nontopological_fqfts_especially_conformal Non-topological FQFTs (especially conformal)
          %p
            This mostly concentrates on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} topological quantum field theories
          %p This was realized in
          %ul
            %li#Segal04
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The definition of conformal field theory
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topology, Geometry and quantum field theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrike+Tillmann"} Ulrike Tillmann
          %p
            There the notion of 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            is axiomatized as a functor on a category of 2-dimensional cobordisms with conformal structure.
          %p (Apparently a similar definition has been given by Kontsevich, but never published.) The details of the category of conformal cobordisms can get a bit technical and slight variations of Segal’s original definition may be necessary. The work by Huang and Kong can be regarded as a further refinement and maybe completion of Segal’s program
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Yi-Zhi Huang,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric interpretation of vertex operator algebras
            %li
              %p
                Liang Kong,
                %em Open-closed field algebras
                Commun. Math. Physics. 280, 207-261 (2008) (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610293"}> arXiv
                ).
          %p
            A very concrete construction of functorial CFTs (for the special case of
            %em rational
            CFTs) is provided by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS-formalism"} FFRS-formalism
          %h3#Extended Extended (multi-tiered) FQFT
          %p
            But one notices that the formalization of quantum field theory as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            on
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            is allowed to be chopped into arbitrary parts of arbitrary co-dimension such that the path integral can still consistently be evaluated on each of these parts.
          %p
            This leads to the notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %em extended quantum field theory
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} infinity category
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+cobordism"} extended cobordism
          %ul
            %li#BaezDolan95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Dolan"} James Dolan
              %em Higher-dimensional algebra and Topological Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9503002"}> arXiv
              ) .
          %p
            Making this precise involves giving a precise definition of an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              Eugenia Cheng and Nick Gurski,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Towards an
                  = succeed "-category" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/18/10/18-10abs.html"} tac
          %p or
          %ul
            %li
              Marco Grandis,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Collared cospans, cohomotopy and TQFT (Cospans in Algebraic Topology, II)
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.dima.unige.it/~grandis/wCub2.pdf"} pdf
          %p There is a long-term project by Stephan Stolz and Peter Teichner which originally tried to refine Segal’s 1-functorial formulation of conformal field theory to a 2-functorial extended FQFT, as indicated in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephan+Stolz"}> Stephan Stolz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
                %em What is an elliptic object?
                (
                %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~teichner/Papers/Oxford.pdf"}> pdf
                ).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephan+Stolz"}> Stephan Stolz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Supersymmetric field theories and generalized cohomology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} H. Sati
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} U. Schreiber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.0189"} arXiv:1108.0189
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/pspum/083"} doi:10.1090/pspum/083/2742432
          %p
            In 2008, Mike Hopkins and Jacob Lurie claimed (
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/05/hopkinslurie_on_baezdolan.html"}> Hopkins-Lurie on Baez-Dolan
            ) to have found a complete coherent formalization of topological extended FQFT in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,n)-categories
            using an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (infinity,n)-category of cobordisms
          %ul
            %li#Luire09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0905.0465"}> arXiv:0905.0465
              )
          %p An explicit account of this for the 2-dimensional case is presented in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Schommer-Pries"}> Chris Schommer-Pries
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Classification of Two-Dimensional Extended Topological Field Theories
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.1000"} arXiv:1112.1000
          %p see also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+quantum+field+theory"} extended topological quantum field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Damien+Calaque"}> Damien Calaque
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudia+Scheimbauer"} Claudia Scheimbauer
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    A note on the
                    = succeed "-category" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.08906"} arXiv:1509.08906
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2140/agt.2019.19.533"} doi:10.2140/agt.2019.19.533
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christopher+Schommer-Pries"}> Christopher Schommer-Pries
                ,
                %em Invertible topological field theories
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.08029"}> arXiv:1712.08029
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Grady"}> Daniel Grady
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Pavlov"} Dmitri Pavlov
                %em Extended field theories are local
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.01208"}> arXiv:2011.01208
                )
          %p
            Claim of classification of geometric FQFTs (including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            etc.) via a geometric
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
          %ul
            %li#GradyPavlov21
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Grady"}> Daniel Grady
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Pavlov"} Dmitri Pavlov
              %em The geometric cobordism hypothesis
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.01095"}> arXiv:2111.01095
              )
          %p For the case of one-dimensional smooth field theories, see
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"} Konrad Waldorf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Parallel Transport and Functors
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0452"} arXiv:0705.0452
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Berwick-Evans"}> Daniel Berwick-Evans
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Pavlov"} Dmitri Pavlov
                = succeed "(" do
                  %em Smooth one-dimensional topological field theories are vector bundles with connection
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.00967"} arXiv:1501.00967
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Ludewig"}> Matthias Ludewig
                , Augusto Stoffel,
                %em A framework for geometric field theories and their classification in dimension one
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.05721"}> arXiv:2001.05721
                )
          %p
            And for the analogous discussion in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            see also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Benini"}> Marco Benini
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Perin"} Marco Perin
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"} Alexander Schenkel
              %em Smooth 1-dimensional algebraic quantum field theories
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.13808"}> arXiv:2010.13808
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p For a functorial construction of two-dimensional smooth field theories from 2-form U(1)-connections and D-branes:
          %ul
            %li#BunkWaldorf21a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Severin+Bunk"}> Severin Bunk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"} Konrad Waldorf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Transgression of D-branes
                %strong 25
                5 (2021) 1095-1198 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/arXiv:1808.04894"}> arXiv:1808.04894
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.4310/ATMP.2021.v25.n5.a1"} doi:10.4310/ATMP.2021.v25.n5.a1
            %li#BunkWaldorf21b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Severin+Bunk"}> Severin Bunk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"} Konrad Waldorf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Smooth functorial field theories from B-fields and D-branes
                %strong 16
                1 (2021) 75-153 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s40062-020-00272-2"}> doi:10.1007/s40062-020-00272-2
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/arXiv:1911.09990"} arXiv:1911.09990
          %h3#extended_fqft_from_background_fields_models
            (extended) FQFT from background fields:
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p In this context Dan Freed is picking up again his old work on higher algebraic structures in quantum field theory, as described in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
                ,
                %em Quantum Groups from Path Integrals
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9501025"}> arXiv
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
                ,
                %em Higher Algebraic Structures and Quantization
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9212115"}> arXiv
                )
          %p
            where he argued that and how the path integral should assign
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-functorial" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em multi-tiered
            QFT instead of extended QFT.)
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              %em Remarks on Chern-Simons Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.2507"}> arXiv
              ).
          %p Freed’s ideas on how an extended or multi-tiered QFT arises from a path integral coming from a given background field were further formalized in the context of “finite” QFTs in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Willerton"}> Simon Willerton
                ,
                %em The twisted Drinfeld double of a finite group via gerbes and finite groupoids
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0503266"}> arXiv
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Bartlett"}> Bruce Bartlett
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On unitary 2-representations of finite groups and topological quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.3975"} arXiv
          %p
            There are indications that a complete picture of this involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
          %ul
            %li
              Jeffrey Morton,
              %em Extended TQFTs and Quantum Gravity
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.0032"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            and, more generally
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric function theory
          %p
            a big advancement in the understanding of extended
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %ul
            %li
              David Ben-Zvi, John Francis, David Nadler,
              %em Integral Transforms and Drinfeld Centers in Derived Geometry
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.0157"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            which realizes
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} infinity-stack
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric functions
          %p A similar approach to bring the old work by Dan Freed mentioned above in contact with the picture of extended functorial QFT and the Baez-Dolan-Lurie structure theorem is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Mike Hopkins
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"} Topological Quantum Field Theories from Compact Lie Groups
          %h3#super_qft Super QFT
          %p See
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+K-theory"} (1,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+tmf"} (2,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and tmf
          %h3#homological_2d_fqft_and_tcft Homological 2d FQFT (and TCFT)
          %p
            As usual, the problem of constructing FQFT becomes much more tractable when linear approximations are applied. In homological FQFT and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            the Hom-spaces of the cobordism category (the moduli spaces of cobordisms with given punctures/boundaries) are approximated by complexes of chains on them. This leads to formalization of
            = succeed "-functorial" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
          %p
            The concept is essentially a formalization of what used to be called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomological+field+theory"} cohomological field theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#Witten91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to cohomological field theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/WittenCQFT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            was given independently by
          %ul
            %li#Getzler92
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ezra+Getzler"}> Ezra Getzler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Batalin-Vilkovisky algebras and two-dimensional topological field theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9212043"} arXiv:hep-th/9212043
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topological field theory
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cgtp.duke.edu/ITP99/segal/"} web
              %a{:href => "http://www.cgtp.duke.edu/ITP99/segal/stanford/lect5.pdf"} lecture 5
              (“topological field theory with cochain values”).
          %p
            The classification of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+A-%E2%88%9E+categories"} Calabi-Yau A-∞ categories
            was discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Costello04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
                ,
                %em Topological conformal field theories and Calabi-Yau categories
                Advances in Mathematics, Volume 210, Issue 1, (2007), (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0412149"}> arXiv:math/0412149
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
                ,
                %em The Gromov-Witten potential associated to a TCFT
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0509264"}> arXiv:math/0509264
                )
          %p
            following conjectures by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homological algebra of mirror symmetry
          %div
            %h3#2dCFTAsFunctorialQFTReferences
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=2
              CFT as functorial field theory
            %p
              Discussion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D2+conformal+field+theory"} D=2 conformal field theory
              as a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+field+theory"} functorial field theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              from a 2d
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+cobordism+category"} conformal cobordism category
              to
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            %ul
              %li#Segal88
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The definition of conformal field theory
                %em Differential geometrical methods in theoretical physics
                (Proceedings of Research Workshop, Como 1987), NATO Adv. Sci. Inst., Ser. C: Math. Phys. Sci.
                %strong 250
                Kluwer Acad. Publ., Dordrecht (1988) 165-171
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-015-7809-7"} doi:10.1007/978-94-015-7809-7
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %p
              and including discussion of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functors"} modular functors
            %ul
              %li#Segal89
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Two-dimensional conformal field theories and modular functors
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Proceedings of the IXth International Congress on Mathematical Physics
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "https://dmitripavlov.org/scans/segal-two-dimensional-conformal-field-theories-and-modular-functors.pdf"} PDF
              %li#Segal04
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em The definition of conformal field theory
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrike+Tillmann"} Ulrike Tillmann
                  (ed.),
                  %em Topology, geometry and quantum field theory
                  , London Math. Soc. Lect. Note Ser.
                  = succeed "," do
                    %strong 308
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511526398.019"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511526398.019
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/segalg/0521540496txt.pdf"} pdf
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SegalDefinitionCFT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %p
              General construction for the case of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+2d+conformal+field+theory"} rational 2d conformal field theory
              is given by the
            %ul
              %li
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS-theorem+on+rational+2d+CFT"} FRS-theorem on rational 2d CFT
            %p See also:
            %ul
              %li#MooreSegal06
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"}> Greg Moore
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
                  %em D-branes and K-theory in 2D topological field theory
                  (
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0609042"}> arXiv:hep-th/0609042
                  )
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Blute"}> Richard Blute
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prakash+Panangaden"} Prakash Panangaden
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorette+Pronk"} Dorette Pronk
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Conformal field theory as a nuclear functor
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "http://aix1.uottawa.ca/~rblute/conf.pdf"} pdf
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2007.02.005"} doi:10.1016/j.entcs.2007.02.005
            %p
              A different but closely analogous development for chiral 2d CFT (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebras"}> vertex operator algebras
              , see
              %a{:href => "vertex+operator+algebra#AsOperadAlgebras"} there
              for more):
            %ul
              %li#Huang91
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yi-Zhi+Huang"}> Yi-Zhi Huang
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Geometric interpretation of vertex operator algebras
                  %strong 88
                  (1991) pp. 9964-9968 (
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.88.22.9964"}> doi:10.1073/pnas.88.22.9964
                  )
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+E.+Tener"}> James E. Tener
                  \:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Representation theory in chiral conformal field theory: from fields to observables
                  %strong 25
                  (2019) 76 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00029-019-0526-3"}> doi:10.1007/s00029-019-0526-3
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.08168"} arXiv:1810.08168
            %p
              Discussion of the case of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+theory"} Liouville theory
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+Guillarmou"}> Colin Guillarmou
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Antti+Kupiainen"} Antti Kupiainen
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R%C3%A9mi+Rhodes"} Rémi Rhodes
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vincent+Vargas"} Vincent Vargas
                  %em Segal’s axioms and bootstrap for Liouville Theory
                  [
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.14859"}> arXiv:2112.14859
                  ]
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hairer"}> Martin Hairer
                  \:
                  %em Probabilistic interpretation of quantum field theories
                  [
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14361"}> arXiv:2501.14361
                  ]
            %p
              Early suggestions to refine this to an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFT"} extended
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functorial
              construction:
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Stolz"}> Stefan Stolz
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/What+is+an+elliptic+object%3F"} What is an elliptic object?
                (2004)
            %p
              A step towards generalization to
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+super-conformal+field+theory"} 2d super-conformal field theory
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephan+Stolz"}> Stephan Stolz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Supersymmetric field theories and generalized cohomology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} H. Sati
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} U. Schreiber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.0189"} arXiv:1108.0189
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/pspum/083"} doi:10.1090/pspum/083/2742432
            %p Discussion of 2-functorial chiral 2d CFT:
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Henriques"}> André Henriques
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The complex cobordism 2-category
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://andreghenriques.com/ComplexCob2CatandCentralExt.mp4"} video
          %div
            %h3#TheoryXAsAnFQFTReferences
              The
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mn 6
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=6
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒩
                    %mo =
